1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
Welcome to Learning Plan 3 and Topic 3.

2
00:00:03,450 --> 00:00:05,050
1, How DNS Works.

3
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:09,300
You find out why networks need name
resolution at all.

4
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:15,950
You learn to read a fully qualified domain
name and to picture the DNS tree behind

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,400
it.

6
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:24,450
You follow a query from the cache on a
Windows client up to the root of the

7
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:25,900
internet.

8
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:31,750
Then you get hands-on: you manage the
client cache, install the DNS Server role

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,800
on SRV01, tour DNS Manager, and test names
with nslookup and Resolve-DnsName.

10
00:00:37,050 --> 00:00:38,250
People remember names.

11
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:40,100
Packets need IP addresses.

12
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:42,750
So something has to translate srv01.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,200
lakeshore.

14
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:45,650
test into 192.

15
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:47,100
168.

16
00:00:47,350 --> 00:00:48,550
50.

17
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:50,000
20.

18
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:51,450
In Lab 2.

19
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:57,300
3 you did that with the hosts file, a text
file on one computer.

20
00:00:57,550 --> 00:01:00,350
But every computer has its own copy.

21
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:06,600
Ten computers means ten files to edit, and
one old line gives a wrong answer.

22
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:12,450
DNS, the Domain Name System, keeps names
on servers instead, and clients just ask.

23
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,300
Change a record once, and every client
sees it.

24
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:20,550
When a service moves, its name can stay
the same.

25
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:22,800
Let's take a name apart.

26
00:01:23,050 --> 00:01:24,250
In srv01.

27
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:25,700
lakeshore.

28
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:29,550
test, each part between the dots is a
label.

29
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,200
Read it from right to left.

30
00:01:32,450 --> 00:01:37,250
At the very end is a dot you usually do
not type.

31
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,700
That dot is the root of the tree.

32
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:43,350
Next comes test, the top-level domain.

33
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:47,200
Then lakeshore, the domain, and finally
srv01, the host.

34
00:01:47,450 --> 00:01:53,450
The full name, down to the root, is a
fully qualified domain name, or FQDN.

35
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:59,700
And test is reserved for testing, so our
practice names never clash with real ones.

36
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:03,550
When CLIENT01 needs an address, where does
it look?

37
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:06,200
First, in its own resolver cache.

38
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:11,650
That cache holds recent answers, and
Windows also loads the hosts file lines

39
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:13,100
into it.

40
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:18,150
If there is no answer, the client asks its
DNS server, SRV01.

41
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:23,200
SRV01 checks the zones it holds; a match
is an authoritative answer.

42
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:25,850
Then it checks its own cache.

43
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:27,300
Still nothing?

44
00:02:27,550 --> 00:02:31,950
It asks forwarders, if any are set, or
uses root hints.

45
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:33,800
The first answer wins.

46
00:02:34,050 --> 00:02:37,650
In the pod, that last step fails: no
internet.

47
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:40,700
Two kinds of query are at work.

48
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:42,950
CLIENT01 sends a recursive query.

49
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:47,200
It means: give me the final answer, or an
error.

50
00:02:47,450 --> 00:02:49,850
The client asks once and waits.

51
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:52,900
SRV01 does the legwork with iterative
queries.

52
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:56,750
A root server says, ask the servers for
test.

53
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,800
Those say, ask the server for lakeshore.

54
00:03:00,050 --> 00:03:01,250
test.

55
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:03,500
That server gives the address.

56
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:08,150
Each step is a referral, a pointer closer
to the name.

57
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:10,400
Then SRV01 answers the client.

58
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:15,450
This is how the internet works; our pod
has no root servers.

59
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:20,500
Every DNS record has a name, a TTL, a
type, and data.

60
00:03:20,750 --> 00:03:23,150
Type A means an IPv4 address.

61
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:27,800
An authoritative answer comes from the
server that holds the zone.

62
00:03:28,050 --> 00:03:30,450
It is the source of truth.

63
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:36,300
A cached answer is a copy from an earlier
lookup, and nslookup calls it

64
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:37,750
non-authoritative.

65
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,200
It is fast, but it can be old.

66
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:47,850
The TTL, or time to live, is how many
seconds a cache may keep that copy.

67
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:54,100
Windows also caches name does not exist
answers, so clear the cache after you fix

68
00:03:54,350 --> 00:03:55,550
a record.

69
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:58,600
You can look inside the client cache.

70
00:03:58,850 --> 00:04:01,650
ipconfig slash displaydns lists every
cached name.

71
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:03,900
Here the entry for srv-core.

72
00:04:04,150 --> 00:04:05,350
lakeshore.

73
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:08,400
test came from a hosts file line.

74
00:04:08,650 --> 00:04:11,450
Record type 1 means an A record.

75
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:14,100
ipconfig slash flushdns clears the cache.

76
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:15,550
But look closely.

77
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:18,600
After the flush, Get-DnsClientCache still
shows srv-core.

78
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:23,650
Flushing removes answers learned from DNS
servers, then Windows loads the hosts

79
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:25,100
lines again.

80
00:04:25,350 --> 00:04:28,550
To remove a hosts answer, edit the file.

81
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:31,600
Clear-DnsClientCache is the PowerShell way
to flush.

82
00:04:31,850 --> 00:04:34,250
Use an administrator window for these.

83
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:38,900
A client only asks a DNS server that it
knows about.

84
00:04:39,150 --> 00:04:40,750
On CLIENT01, open ncpa.

85
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
cpl, then the Ethernet properties, then
Internet Protocol Version 4.

86
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:48,050
The IP address stays as it is.

87
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:51,900
Under Use the following DNS server
addresses, type 192.

88
00:04:52,150 --> 00:04:53,350
168.

89
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:54,800
50.

90
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:57,450
20 as the preferred DNS server.

91
00:04:57,700 --> 00:04:58,900
That is SRV01.

92
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:05,150
The alternate server is only asked when
the first one does not answer, and we

93
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:06,600
leave it empty.

94
00:05:06,850 --> 00:05:10,050
Windows 11 Settings can change the same
value.

95
00:05:10,300 --> 00:05:13,100
In PowerShell, Set-DnsClientServerAddress
does it, and Get-DnsClientServerAddress

96
00:05:13,350 --> 00:05:14,550
reads it back.

97
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:16,400
Now the server side.

98
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:21,450
On SRV01, open Server Manager, then
Manage, then Add Roles and Features.

99
00:05:21,700 --> 00:05:24,500
You know this wizard from Topic 2.

100
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:25,950
1.

101
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:28,200
On Server Selection, pick SRV01.

102
00:05:28,450 --> 00:05:34,050
On Server Roles, select DNS Server, then
Add Features to get the management tools.

103
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:39,100
The wizard adds a DNS Server page; read it
and select Next.

104
00:05:39,350 --> 00:05:40,550
Then Install.

105
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:42,400
No restart is needed.

106
00:05:42,650 --> 00:05:48,650
A DNS server needs a static IP address,
because clients point at it by address.

107
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:51,700
SRV01 has had one since Lab 1.

108
00:05:51,950 --> 00:05:53,150
4.

109
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:56,600
Here is the same job in one line.

110
00:05:56,850 --> 00:05:59,250
Install-WindowsFeature, dash Name DNS,
dash IncludeManagementTools.

111
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:03,100
The result says Success, and Restart
Needed says No.

112
00:06:03,350 --> 00:06:07,350
The feature result lists DNS Server and
DNS Server Tools.

113
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,800
To check, run Get-WindowsFeature for DNS
and RSAT-DNS-Server.

114
00:06:11,050 --> 00:06:13,850
An X in the brackets means installed.

115
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:18,900
Finally, Get-Service shows the service
named DNS, with the display name DNS

116
00:06:19,150 --> 00:06:20,350
Server, is running.

117
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:24,200
It is running, but it has no zones yet.

118
00:06:24,450 --> 00:06:29,250
Keep that in mind, because it decides what
this server can answer.

119
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:32,700
Open DNS Manager from Server Manager,
Tools, DNS.

120
00:06:32,950 --> 00:06:34,950
The left pane shows SRV01.

121
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:40,400
Under it, Forward Lookup Zones turn names
into addresses, and Reverse Lookup Zones

122
00:06:40,650 --> 00:06:42,650
turn addresses back into names.

123
00:06:42,900 --> 00:06:44,500
Both are empty today.

124
00:06:44,750 --> 00:06:50,750
Conditional Forwarders is where you send
one domain to a specific server, in Lab 3.

125
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:52,200
3.

126
00:06:52,450 --> 00:06:58,450
Global Logs holds DNS Events, the DNS
Server log, the first place to look when

127
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:00,700
the service has a problem.

128
00:07:00,950 --> 00:07:04,950
To see the server settings, right-click
SRV01 and choose Properties.

129
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:09,600
The Properties dialog has seven tabs;
domain controllers add an eighth,

130
00:07:09,850 --> 00:07:11,050
Security.

131
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:12,500
Four matter today.

132
00:07:12,750 --> 00:07:16,750
Interfaces shows that the server listens
on all IP addresses.

133
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:18,200
Keep that.

134
00:07:18,450 --> 00:07:24,050
Forwarders is empty, and Use root hints if
no forwarders are available is checked.

135
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:29,900
On Advanced, Disable recursion is off, so
SRV01 looks up names for its clients.

136
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:34,950
Root Hints lists the thirteen root
servers, a dot root-servers dot net

137
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:36,400
through m.

138
00:07:36,650 --> 00:07:42,650
They are built into Windows, and they are
real internet servers, so our pod cannot

139
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:44,100
reach them.

140
00:07:44,350 --> 00:07:45,550
Put it together.

141
00:07:45,800 --> 00:07:51,000
SRV01 runs DNS but holds no zones, so it
is a caching-only server.

142
00:07:51,250 --> 00:07:53,650
It can only recurse and cache.

143
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:58,300
In the pod, recursion leads to root
servers it cannot reach.

144
00:07:58,550 --> 00:08:00,150
So every name fails.

145
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:04,400
It may fail after several seconds of
retries, or quickly.

146
00:08:04,650 --> 00:08:07,850
That is a timeout, or a server failure.

147
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:11,300
NXDOMAIN is different: DNS name does not
exist.

148
00:08:11,550 --> 00:08:16,750
That is a real, fast answer from the
server that holds the zone.

149
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,200
A timeout points at the network or server.

150
00:08:20,450 --> 00:08:22,450
Two tools test DNS itself.

151
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:27,900
nslookup asks the DNS server directly,
skipping the hosts file and the cache.

152
00:08:28,150 --> 00:08:29,350
In Lab 3.

153
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:35,200
1 it shows Server UnKnown, because SRV01
has no reverse record yet; Lab 3.

154
00:08:35,450 --> 00:08:36,650
2 fixes that.

155
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:38,900
Then the request times out.

156
00:08:39,150 --> 00:08:43,950
Type nslookup alone for an interactive
prompt, and exit to leave it.

157
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:46,200
Resolve-DnsName is the PowerShell tool.

158
00:08:46,450 --> 00:08:52,450
Dash Name picks the name, dash Type the
record type, and dash Server the server.

159
00:08:52,700 --> 00:08:58,300
Dash DnsOnly rules out LLMNR and NetBIOS,
and dash NoHostsFile skips the hosts file.

160
00:08:58,550 --> 00:09:00,150
A quick look ahead.

161
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:05,600
In this learning plan, zones live in text
files on SRV01 and SRV-CORE.

162
00:09:05,850 --> 00:09:11,050
One server holds the primary copy, and
secondaries get read-only copies by zone

163
00:09:11,300 --> 00:09:12,500
transfer.

164
00:09:12,750 --> 00:09:13,950
In Topic 4.

165
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:17,400
1 you meet Active Directory-integrated
zones on DC01.

166
00:09:17,650 --> 00:09:22,850
They are stored in the Active Directory
database and copied by AD replication.

167
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:27,900
They also allow secure dynamic updates, so
only domain members update records.

168
00:09:28,150 --> 00:09:29,350
Why files now?

169
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:34,000
Our servers are in a workgroup, with no
domain controller yet.

170
00:09:34,250 --> 00:09:35,850
Four mistakes to avoid.

171
00:09:36,100 --> 00:09:39,300
One: testing DNS with ping or a browser.

172
00:09:39,550 --> 00:09:44,350
They also use the hosts file, the cache
and local name protocols.

173
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:48,600
Use nslookup, or Resolve-DnsName with dash
DnsOnly and dash NoHostsFile.

174
00:09:48,850 --> 00:09:52,050
Two: leaving test lines in the hosts file.

175
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:57,900
A hosts line wins over DNS, so remove it
once DNS has the record.

176
00:09:58,150 --> 00:10:00,950
Three: testing right after fixing a
record.

177
00:10:01,200 --> 00:10:04,800
The old answer may be cached, so flush
first.

178
00:10:05,050 --> 00:10:08,650
Four: reading a timeout as name does not
exist.

179
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:11,300
Check the server and network first.

180
00:10:11,550 --> 00:10:13,150
Time to check yourself.

181
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:16,200
CLIENT01 uses SRV01 as its DNS server.

182
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:20,050
First: CLIENT01's hosts file gives one
address for srv-core.

183
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:21,500
lakeshore.

184
00:10:21,750 --> 00:10:23,750
test, but DNS has another.

185
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
Which address does ping use?

186
00:10:26,250 --> 00:10:29,850
Second: ping for that name works, but
nslookup fails.

187
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:31,300
Why?

188
00:10:31,550 --> 00:10:33,150
Third: Resolve-DnsName for www.

189
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:34,600
microsoft.

190
00:10:34,850 --> 00:10:36,050
com fails.

191
00:10:36,300 --> 00:10:41,100
Is that a name does not exist answer,
called NXDOMAIN, and why?

192
00:10:41,350 --> 00:10:45,750
And fourth: SRV01 has the DNS Server role
but no zones.

193
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,800
What kind of DNS server is it?

194
00:10:49,050 --> 00:10:51,850
Pause the video and think it through.

195
00:10:52,100 --> 00:10:53,700
Here are the answers.

196
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:56,750
One: ping uses the hosts file address.

197
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:02,600
Hosts lines are loaded into the resolver
cache, which is checked before any DNS

198
00:11:02,850 --> 00:11:04,050
server.

199
00:11:04,300 --> 00:11:09,900
Two: nslookup asks the DNS server directly
and skips the hosts file and cache.

200
00:11:10,150 --> 00:11:14,550
Ping used the hosts line; the DNS server
has no record.

201
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:16,000
Three: no.

202
00:11:16,250 --> 00:11:21,850
It is a timeout or server failure, because
SRV01 cannot reach the root servers

203
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:23,300
without internet.

204
00:11:23,550 --> 00:11:25,150
Four: a caching-only server.

205
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:29,000
It is authoritative for nothing until you
add zones.

206
00:11:29,250 --> 00:11:30,850
Let me show you.

207
00:11:31,100 --> 00:11:35,900
On CLIENT01, in an administrator
PowerShell, I add a hosts line for

208
00:11:36,150 --> 00:11:37,350
srv-core.

209
00:11:37,600 --> 00:11:38,800
lakeshore.

210
00:11:39,050 --> 00:11:40,250
test.

211
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:42,900
ipconfig slash displaydns already lists
it.

212
00:11:43,150 --> 00:11:45,950
I flush, and it is still there.

213
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:49,400
On SRV01, I add the DNS Server role.

214
00:11:49,650 --> 00:11:54,050
DNS Manager shows empty zone folders, root
hints and no forwarders.

215
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:58,300
Back on CLIENT01, I set the DNS server to
SRV01.

216
00:11:58,550 --> 00:11:59,750
www.

217
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,200
microsoft.

218
00:12:01,450 --> 00:12:03,450
com fails with a timeout.

219
00:12:03,700 --> 00:12:05,300
nslookup says Server UnKnown.

220
00:12:05,550 --> 00:12:09,550
And srv-core answers only while the hosts
file is used.

221
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:12,600
With dash NoHostsFile, it times out too.

222
00:12:12,850 --> 00:12:14,050
To wrap up.

223
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:16,300
DNS turns names into addresses.

224
00:12:16,550 --> 00:12:21,750
A fully qualified domain name reads from
the host up to the root.

225
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:27,600
Windows checks its cache, with the hosts
lines, then its DNS server, which checks

226
00:12:27,850 --> 00:12:31,050
zones and cache, then forwarders or root
hints.

227
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:36,900
Authoritative answers come from the zone;
cached copies live as long as the TTL.

228
00:12:37,150 --> 00:12:42,750
A server with no zones is caching-only,
and with no internet you get timeouts.

229
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:44,200
Lab 3.

230
00:12:44,450 --> 00:12:48,050
1 takes about forty-five minutes and is
practice only.

231
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:49,500
See you there.
